    "translation": "The rapid spread of generative AI is confronting schools with a serious question. In an age when students can obtain answers to assignments in an instant, how much meaning is left in conventional homework and exams? Some educators argue that AI use should be banned outright, and quite a few schools do prohibit it. Overseas, software has even appeared that judges whether a submitted text was written by AI. The desire to prevent cheating is not entirely incomprehensible.\nHowever, a response consisting of nothing but prohibition risks making us lose sight of the very essence of education. The purpose of education is not the memorization of knowledge itself, but the cultivation of the ability to scrutinize information and think with one's own mind. Just as the arrival of the calculator did not destroy mathematics education, the appearance of a new tool can instead be an opportunity to rethink, from the ground up, what we should be teaching. Merely keeping the tool at arm's length only widens the gap between the classroom and the reality students will face once they enter society.\nThat said, this does not mean we should accept AI unconditionally. If students who have not yet built basic thinking skills develop the habit of obtaining only answers, the process of thinking itself may be hollowed out. Indeed, examples of AI blithely presenting plausible-sounding misinformation are too numerous to count. What is urgently needed is to cultivate a critical eye that can detect AI's errors rather than swallowing its output whole.\nIn short, what is being questioned is not the rightness or wrongness of the technology but the imaginative capacity of education itself. Rather than fleeing into the binary of prohibition versus acceptance, we must design an education that trains thinking while coexisting with the new tool. The responsibility for producing that answer lies not with the technology, but with us adults.",
