    "translation": "At train stations and commercial facilities, the introduction of facial recognition technology is advancing rapidly. You pass through the ticket gate with just your face and finish your shopping without taking out your wallet. Combined with security cameras, it is also powerful in criminal investigations, and its convenience and contribution to safety are beyond doubt. Applications have spread to airport immigration checks and event admissions, and the lines have grown visibly shorter.\nYet this technology makes it possible to collect biometric information without a person's knowledge and to track an individual's movements continuously. Unlike a fingerprint, a face cannot be hidden and can be read from a distance without consent — therein lies this technology's peculiar character. The refrain 'If you have nothing to hide, being watched is no problem' remains deep-rooted. But this view overlooks the effect surveillance has on the inner life. The awareness of being constantly watched steers people toward inoffensive behavior and causes free action and expression to shrivel from within. Precisely because that shriveling brings no visible harm, it goes all the more unnoticed.\nEven so, it would be simplistic to brand the technology itself as evil and cry for its elimination. The heart of the problem is not the technology but the absence of norms and institutions to govern its use. Who collects what range of information, and for what purpose? Principles such as notification and consent, and the prohibition of use beyond the stated purpose, must be clearly established in law. In Europe, in fact, legislation strictly limiting the use of facial recognition in public spaces is already ahead of us.\nWhat are we handing over in exchange for convenience? Sharing that question across society and building a framework that enjoys the technology's benefits while preventing its abuse — that is the task that must be urgently addressed. For even while we postpone the debate, the technology keeps seeping into every corner of society.",
