    "translation": "When I spotted the faded sign for the Kinoshita Piano School at the edge of the shopping street, my reflex was to walk faster. Thirty years ago, the student who quit the school right before a competition without giving any reason — that was me. Too afraid to imagine my teacher's crestfallen face, I had come all the way to today without managing to write her so much as a single letter. Even as an adult, whenever the sound of a piano reached my ears, something deep in my chest gave a faint ache.\nBut my feet, against my will, stopped in front of the sign. From behind the glass door came a halting practice piece — the same piece I had first stumbled over back then. Clumsy as the notes were, they kept struggling earnestly forward. Before I knew it I was pulling the door open. My teacher, her hair now white, looked at me for a while over her reading glasses, and then said: 'Ah. The child with the strong left hand.'\nThere was no time even to prepare words of apology. As if it were the most natural thing in the world, she showed me into her sitting room, never once touched on how I had quit, and said she had loved the lullaby I used to play. Not the competition set piece — she had remembered that small, unimportant tune I used to play on a whim at the end of lessons. The hands holding her teacup had grown small and wrinkled, but the shape of her nails was exactly as I remembered. The title I had carried for thirty years — 'the student who betrayed her' — had never existed in her memory at all.\nAs I was leaving she said: come again — next time, to play. Walking through the bustle of the shopping street, I finally understood who had been accusing me for thirty years. It was not my teacher. It was me. I felt I had at last arrived at a place where the long, heavy burden could be set down.",
