    "translation": "The history of technology has also been a history of war against waiting time. Letters became e-mail, photographs can be checked on the spot, and anything we want to know comes back in seconds if we search. Waiting has been removed from life, item by item, as waste to be trimmed. No one doubted that direction.\nBut when waiting time disappears, is nothing disappearing along with it? During the days spent waiting for a reply, people reread the other person's words again and again and re-examined their own feelings. The week spent waiting for film to be developed let the memory of the moment of shooting mature. Waiting was not a mere blank; it was time in which meaning grew. In a life where what we want is obtained instantly, both the anticipation that should have swelled beforehand and the afterglow that should have followed are stretched thin.\nThat said, I am not saying we should return to the inconvenient past. There is no reason whatsoever to give up the blessing of urgent messages arriving in an instant. The problem is not speed itself, but that the experience of waiting is being swept out of every domain, so that being unable to wait becomes the norm. When we grow irritated at a few seconds of loading, our sense of time is unmistakably being remade.\nWaiting is also training in accepting time that does not bend to our will. A person who has sown seeds cannot order the soil to sprout faster. Raising a person, building trust — these too should be inseparable from waiting. A society that has lost the power to wait will grow cold toward everything that takes time to mature.",
