    "translation": "No one can fully explain in words how to ride a bicycle. We ride without being able to explain it, and a person who cannot ride will not learn to by listening to explanations. The same goes for swimming, and for the pronunciation of our mother tongue. Some knowledge can be put into words; some dwells only in the body. The philosopher Polanyi called the latter tacit knowledge.\nA seasoned baker, they say, knows how far fermentation has progressed the instant he touches the dough. The feel of the palm is more accurate than the numbers on a thermometer or a clock. But asked 'How do you know?', the craftsman falls silent. The same can be said of the hand that sharpens a kitchen knife or the speed at which a brush is moved. If years of apprenticeship begin not with explanations to the pupil but with imitation, it is precisely because the core of what must be transmitted lies outside language. However precisely a manual is written, the final step is always left out.\nThat said, tacit knowledge should not be mystified. Before the hand remembers, there are countless failures and repetitions. A craftsman's intuition is not an inborn gift but another name for the vast trial and error deposited in the body. If anything, mystification risks becoming an excuse to skip the patient practice.\nAn age that prizes efficiency tends to count as knowledge only what can be turned into words or data. But if we preserve only what can be documented, the deepest layer of technique will quietly be lost. It is this layer, in fact, that machines find hardest to replace. To pay respect to what the hand knows is also to protect the whole of human knowing.",
