    "translation": "'Easy to understand' has at some point become the highest praise. Easy-to-understand lessons, easy-to-understand explanations, easy-to-understand books. Summarize the key points in three, state the conclusion first, attach a diagram. As effort on the communicator's part, I have no intention of condemning this in itself. But when comprehensibility becomes the sole yardstick, is something not being quietly damaged?\nAn easy explanation is also a trimming-down of its subject to fit the framework the receiver already has. Philosophy-in-three-minutes is only the three-minutes' worth extracted from philosophy. The world itself has not become simpler. The chance to train ourselves to receive complex things in their complexity is being lost in the shadow of the summary. Worse, the pleasant feeling of having understood closely resembles the grip of real understanding. So people drift toward speakers who make them 'get it' faster, and dismiss speakers who make them think slowly as 'hard to follow.'\nIf anything, the experience of not understanding right away is the very gateway to knowledge. A part that does not sit right stays in the mind, and one day it connects with some other experience and suddenly opens up. That slow ignition happens precisely because the initial 'not-understanding' was preserved. Instant satisfaction defuses this time bomb.\nThat said, we must not fall into the perversion of thinking that whatever is obscure is therefore deep. Needlessly ornamented writing is laziness disguising simplicity. What should be asked is not whether something is easy or hard, but whether it is faithful to the complexity of its subject. Faithful telling sometimes keeps the reader waiting. I want to be a reader who accepts that waiting time along with the rest.",
