    "translation": "Standing before a masterpiece of ink painting, one's eye is sometimes drawn less to the painted pines and boats than to the parts where nothing is painted. The white of the paper is not mere background. It is mist, it is the water's surface, it is a body of water in which the viewer's imagination can swim. To paint by not painting — this paradox touches the core not just of ink painting but of expression as such.\nMusicians are said to treasure rests as much as notes. Rakugo storytellers say the contest is decided in the instant of silence just before the audience laughs. Crammed-in information passes straight through the receiver; a well-placed blank draws the receiver in. That is because a blank leaves work for the receiver. When people fill in the omitted parts themselves, they change from mere spectators into co-creators. This is surely why an untold ending stays in the heart longer than a story in which everything is explained.\nThat said, blank space is no excuse for cutting corners. Immature omission is just insufficient explanation. A master's blank space is eloquent because what to depict and what to leave undepicted is controlled down to the finest detail. Blank space is not leftover empty area; it is silence deliberately carved in.\nA planner filled with appointments, a screen updating without a gap. Blank space is disappearing from our lives. But what has been filled to the brim can receive nothing more. A day packed with appointments and information leaves no room for chance to slip in. Just as an empty day in the calendar invites an unexpected encounter, blank space is another name for possibility. To design a life is to decide not only what to fill it with, but where to leave it open.",
