    "translation": "A person accustomed to a white cane, it is said, feels the bumps of the road surface not in the palm gripping the cane but at the cane's tip. For a skilled carpenter, the plane is not an object to be consciously operated but an extension of the hand. Glasses, chopsticks, bicycles, keyboards. A well-worn tool becomes part of the body precisely by vanishing from consciousness.\nConversely, a tool rises into consciousness when it stops working well. A pen first appears before us as a 'thing' the moment its ink runs out. In philosophy this is sometimes called the transparency of tools. The difference between a beginner and an expert, more than a difference in the amount of knowledge, is a difference in how transparent the tool has become. In a beginner's fingers, chopsticks are a foreign object that thrashes about; an expert's chopsticks sense the softness of tofu as if they were fingertips.\nFrom this viewpoint, the boundary of the body does not end at the skin. Indeed, the smartphone is already turning into an external organ that shoulders our memory and sense of direction. And yet the more transparent a tool becomes, the harder it is for us to be aware of our dependence on it. Only when the battery dies do we realize how much we had entrusted to that small slab. Many people report that they stopped remembering routes once they began using a map app. An ability we have entrusted can be lost along with the tool.\nChoosing a tool, then, is not simply choosing convenience. It is deciding how far to extend the outline of one's body and mind, and what to entrust where. The inventory of tools that fit one's hand is, as it stands, a map of that person's abilities. To think about tools is nothing other than to think about what the self is.",
