    "translation": "Now that a search returns an answer in seconds, it is often said that memorizing knowledge has lost its value. But is it not questions, rather than answers, that have truly become scarce? Answers are given from outside; only the question must be made inside one's own head, before anything is typed into the search box.\nA good question rearranges the field of view. The person who asks 'How can we make this sell?' and the person who asks 'Whose problem, and which one, are we trying to solve in the first place?' are looking at different landscapes even in the same meeting room. An answer is like liquid poured into the vessel of a question. If the vessel is warped, however precise the answer, it hardens warped. Nothing leads people astray like an accurate answer to a mistaken question. Yet we fret only over the correctness of answers and almost never doubt the shape of the vessel. A child asks 'Why is the sky blue?' without a second thought, but as we become adults, asking turns into a shameful 'confession of ignorance.'\nThat said, not just any question will do. Questions asked to test someone, or to feign thought, stop thinking rather than start it. A good question has only one condition: that the asker stands ready to change their own thinking in light of the answer.\nNow that machines have taken over the generating of answers, the power to ask returns to the center of intelligence. For the machine only answers what it is asked; it does not tell us what we ought to ask. What we choose to ask is a declaration of what we hold important. To frame a question is nothing other than to decide one's own stance toward the world.",
