    "translation": "Is it a coincidence that so many philosophers were fond of walking? Kant walked the same route at the same hour every day, and in Kyoto there remains a lane called the 'Path of Philosophy' after the meditations of Nishida Kitaro. Why do people walk in order to think?\nA problem that has hit a dead end at the desk sometimes unravels the moment we start walking. The monotonous rhythm of walking steadies the breath and loosens, just slightly, the reins of consciousness. Then associations that were held down at the desk begin to move of their own accord, and unexpected combinations are born. At the speed of a car or a train, the scenery merely streams away, overtaking thought. The pace of walking, it seems, is somewhere in balance with the pace at which thoughts ripen.\nWhat is more, walking can cease to be movement solely for the sake of a destination. Movement with nothing in mind but arriving is mere consumption of time. But when we walk with no particular aim, we are moving yet hurrying nowhere. This suspended state is a rare time within daily life when the mind is released from duties and roles.\nThat said, there is no guarantee anywhere that walking will always produce a bright idea. Even so, I think: to keep, if only briefly each day, a time when we step out of the race of speed. To lift our eyes from the screen and feel the ground under the soles of our feet. When thinking stalls, first to stand up from the chair. For the instruments of thought are not the head alone.",
