    "translation": "Everyone has surely had the experience of being startled by a recording of their own voice: 'Is this what I sound like?' We often feel the same dissonance at our own figure in a photograph. The self we should know best is in fact the self we see least. Come to think of it, we know our own face only through the mirror — a left-right reversed image.\nOther people are in contact, every day, with my 'real' face and voice. In other words, even the most basic information about myself — my appearance and my voice — is known more accurately by others than by me. And might not the same, surprisingly, be said of the interior? Quickness to anger, verbal tics, the expression I wear when tired. We bristle at a friend's observation — 'That's not true' — and only later realize they were right. A self-image is a map drawn from the inside, and it always diverges somewhere from the actual terrain.\nThat does not mean, however, that others' assessments are always correct. Others, too, see only the me of limited situations, and they see through their own biases. The self-image and the image others hold are mirrors warped in different directions. What is certain is that neither alone is enough.\nKnowing oneself is usually taken to mean deepening one's introspection. But however long we peer inside, all that is there is the reversed mirror. Rather, being startled by the words of someone we trust — refusing to let go of that unpleasant surprise — may be the royal road to self-understanding.",
