    "translation": "For videos, music, and news alike, \"recommended for you\" has become the entrance. Recommendation algorithms that infer your tastes from your browsing history and hand you the next thing to watch are certainly convenient. It is fair to say we avoid drowning in the vast sea of information thanks to this invisible guide. And yet I believe it is precisely this comfort that calls for vigilance.\nThe recommendation mechanism gathers things that resemble your past self. Which means what you encounter there is always a variation on \"what you already liked.\" A continuation of yesterday is served up again today and tomorrow, and the plate is always filled with the same flavor. The kind of chance we once experienced in bookstores and libraries—being stopped in your tracks by an unrelated book on the way to the shelf you came for—is eliminated at the design stage. Your taste seems to be getting refined, but in fact its outline is being fixed in place. What's more, the person themselves has no awareness that their options are being narrowed, because what you were never shown is the same as what does not exist.\nSo what should we do? I am not saying we should hate the algorithm and throw it away. What I want to propose is a habit of deliberately \"straying.\" Once a month, leaf through a magazine in a field you have no interest in. Read something written by someone you would normally never choose. It is also worth stepping into a library in an unfamiliar town, in the spirit of a stroll rather than a search. In short, redesign chance for yourself. Once, chance came to us on its own even if we left things alone. Now chance has become something we can only invite in by force of will. The breadth of our curiosity depends on whether we begrudge that effort.",
