    "translation": "In regional cities whose populations keep shrinking, the phrase \"compact city\" is being chanted like a magic spell. The national government, too, has pushed this vision as a prescription for the age of shrinkage. Gather the housing and commercial facilities that sprawled into the suburbs around the station, and remake the town into one where people can live on foot. Administrative services become more efficient, and elderly people who cannot drive find life easier—the logic sounds plausible. I too agree with the direction itself. However, I have doubts about the way it is currently being pursued.\nMany municipalities' plans begin with drawing lines on a map: please live inside this zone. But a home is not a mere box. It is the totality of a life, including the trees in the garden, relations with neighbors, and the location of the family grave. To think this can be moved with the single word \"efficiency\" can only be called armchair arrogance. In fact, quite a few cities have designated residence-guidance zones only to see housing development continue outside them. Since suburban houses are cheaper and larger, this is, if anything, only a natural result. Drawing lines alone does not move people.\nWhat is needed is a concrete mechanism that makes moving worthwhile. Rent subsidies in the center and support for renovating vacant houses, of course—but also that the everyday footholds, hospitals and shops, are already in place at the destination first. Sequence matters here too: if you demand withdrawal before preparing a receiving base for daily life, you will end up leaving nothing but distrust of the policy. Folding up a town that took thirty years to spread out demands just as much time and persuasion. The compact city is right. But rightness, unless it passes through the consent of the people who live there, remains a mere barked order.",
