    "translation": "When people hear \"measures against the falling birthrate,\" most picture cash benefits—larger child allowances, lump-sum childbirth payments. So many tens of thousands of yen per child, an extra amount for a third child, and so on. Politicians, too, roll out new benefits as if competing over the size of the sums. There is no reason to oppose policies that lighten the burden on household finances. However, I believe the very idea that piling up benefits will restore the birthrate contains a fundamental oversight.\nWhat is overlooked is the problem of time and of the future that the younger generation faces. In workplaces where long hours are the norm, increasing the allowance by any number of yen does not add a single minute to the time spent with one's child. If employment is unstable and you cannot predict your income a few years ahead, there is no way to plan for child-rearing that spans twenty years. If getting into daycare is left to luck, you cannot even sketch a picture of balancing work and family. In other words, what makes people hesitate to have children is not just immediate expense but the way we work and the visibility of the future. Policies that reach the wallet are easy to see and well suited to election publicity. But that is like taking a painkiller and feeling you have treated the cause of the fever.\nTherefore I want to argue that at the center of birthrate policy we should place not benefits but structural reform: ways of working that do not presuppose overtime, workplaces where men can take parental leave without penalty, stable employment. None of these acts quickly, and the results may not show in the numbers for ten years. They sit poorly with the electoral cycle, and it is no wonder politicians prefer to avoid them. But this road that looks like a detour is, in fact, the only shortcut.",
