    "translation": "Remote work, which spread all at once on the occasion of the epidemic, turned toward contraction at many companies after the epidemic passed. With the rallying cry \"we really must meet face to face,\" employees are being called back to packed trains. The papers also feature executives celebrating the resumption of commuting as \"a return to normal life.\" Meanwhile, some companies stick to fully remote work. I side with neither camp, because what should be asked is not the binary of home versus office, but the definition: what is the office a place for?\nWhat several years of remote work proved is the fact that, for tasks completed alone, the office was adding almost nothing. Preparing documents and routine administration hold up fine without spending two hours a day commuting. On the other hand, some things were certainly lost: ideas born of hallway small talk, the newcomer's chance to steal glances at how seniors work, signs of someone struggling caught early in idle chat. All of these are value produced by chance contact, the kind of thing that never appears on a meeting schedule.\nIf so, the conclusion should be clear. The office need only change its role from \"a place you must be every day\" to \"a device for producing chance.\" Days for concentration at home; days for mingling at the company. On office days, rather than cramming in meetings, deliberately leave slack and create time for people to mix. A company that uniformly mandates attendance must be able to explain what it provides in return for the cost it exacts in commuting time. The era of gathering people out of habit is over.",
