    "translation": "The bridges, tunnels, and water mains built all at once during the era of high growth are now reaching the end of their lives together. Bridges judged dangerous in inspections yet left untouched for lack of funds are increasing year by year. Neither the money nor the engineers to rebuild everything remain in this country of shrinking population. Facing this reality, I dare to argue: we should switch our thinking about infrastructure from something to \"protect\" to something to \"fold up\"—and the longer we postpone that triage, the crueler the situation will become.\nI am aware this is not a pleasant-sounding argument. Even a bridge with a few dozen users a day is a lifeline for the people living at its foot. The moment a politician mentions closure, criticism rains down that the region is being abandoned. As a result, what is practiced everywhere is \"equal life-extension\": spreading the limited budget thinly over every facility. But this is nothing other than a choice to repair no bridge adequately—the equivalent of leaving to luck when and where one will fall.\nFolding up is not the same as abandoning. If a bridge is to be closed, promise shared transport to the opposite bank. For a hamlet where the water main cannot be relaid, prepare alternative means—water trucks, small purification units. In short, preserve the function instead of preserving the facility. The beautiful pretense of protecting everything ends up protecting no one. The courage to speak about priorities is what will be demanded of both governments and residents from here on.",
