    "translation": "Restaurants, books, inns, even doctors are now spoken of in numbers of stars. Averages out of five line up in search results, and we choose the four-star place over the three-star one. The merit of a place you have never visited is taught to you, in effect, by the numbers left by those who have. Since this lets us make low-risk choices even in an unfamiliar town, I concede it is a convenient system. Even so, I believe the yardstick of the average score is quietly starving our culture.\nAn average is an operation that flattens divided opinions into a single number. A restaurant where half the customers give five stars and the other half give one star, and a restaurant where everyone gives three stars, stand side by side wearing the same \"three-star\" face. But the former has something that sends a certain kind of person into raptures, and the latter probably has nothing. The story behind the score sinks into the average, out of sight. What a restaurant learns in an age of being chosen by score is not how to move someone deeply but how to anger no one. A sharp individuality is nothing but a risk of lost points, and uniformity under the name of \"inoffensive\" becomes the optimal strategy. And so the town becomes a place where, wherever you eat, it is \"not bad\"—and wherever you eat, nothing stays in your memory.\nSo while I will not tell you not to look at scores, I want to propose changing how we read them. Look not at the average but at how the ratings split. Read the words of the highest and lowest reviews, and ask whether their complaints would be complaints for you. A number is the entrance to judgment, not the exit. When we surrender judgment to the convenience of stars, what is lost is not only the individuality of restaurants. The outline of our own tastes, which was contained in the very act of choosing, fades away as well.",
