    "translation": "As the labor shortage deepens, expanding the acceptance of foreign workers has come to be discussed as a matter of course. Nursing care, construction, agriculture—none of them runs without these workers anymore. Yet look at the design of the system and you find this country steadfastly maintaining a strange pretense: what we need is \"labor,\" not \"immigrants,\" and when the period is up they will be asked to go home. I believe it is precisely this self-deception that produces most of the problems surrounding acceptance.\nA European writer once said: \"We called for labor, but human beings came.\" A working person has a life. They fall ill, they fall in love, children are born. Yet under a system that has decreed their stay to be short-term, investment in Japanese-language education is meager, finding housing is left to private goodwill, and whether a child can enroll in school depends on luck. The premise that \"these people will leave anyway\" pushes back all the preparation needed to live together in a community. People voice unease at the sight of isolated clusters of foreigners, but that isolation is not something they chose; it is something the system prepared for them.\nLately people like to ask whether Japan can become a \"country that gets chosen.\" With the yen weak and the appeal of wages fading, the question is certainly urgent. But what is really being asked is our resolve to treat those who come as neighbors. The self-serving arrangement of receiving only the labor while declining the human being cannot continue. The time has come to place language support, education, and housing—premised on settlement—squarely on the policy agenda.",
