    "translation": "Limited research funds should be concentrated on fields where results can be expected—this policy, known as \"selection and concentration,\" has governed Japanese science policy for the past two decades and more. The base funds distributed to universities across the board have been cut, and every few years researchers are made to promise, on grant application forms, the \"usefulness\" of their research. As an explanation to taxpayers it sounds, at first hearing, reasonable. But I believe this policy fundamentally misreads the nature of the enterprise called science.\nWhat the history of science teaches is that many great discoveries were born from research whose use, at the outset, nobody could see. Research into how deep-sea creatures glow transformed observation techniques in medicine; abstract mathematical theory became cryptography and now protects our daily shopping. No method exists for knowing in advance which seed will bear fruit. Precisely for that reason, sowing widely and holding on to a diversity that looks like waste is, over the long run, the surest investment. The farmer who sows only the seeds that look likely to bear fruit is in fact standing on the arrogance of claiming to prophesy the harvest—and his field is wiped out in the year the weather changes.\nA system that demands promises of results has changed researchers' behavior as well. Small questions certain to become papers get chosen; challenges with a large risk of failure are avoided. Young researchers count their publications within short fixed terms and cannot reach for work that requires settling in. What I want to demand is not the wholesale abandonment of selection and concentration but the restoration of a ratio: thicken once more the base funds that demand no promise of use, and secure within the system room for discoveries that cannot be foretold. That, properly speaking, is the kind of wager an investment in science is.",
