    "translation": "I have lived in Tokyo for twenty years, but on snowy mornings, even now, my body notices first. Before I open the curtains, the light in the room is different from usual: the ceiling glows faintly white. And there is one more decisive sign — the sounds are gone. No cars, no newspaper-delivery motorbike. That peculiar soundlessness, as if the world had been packed with cotton.\nFor me, raised in snow country, snow was something that fell first upon the ears. At night, the sense of it piling up silently reached you not as a sound but as a way sounds disappeared. On the morning walk to school, going down a road with no one's footprints, the only thing that rang out in the world was the squeak of my own boots. Back then, silence was not the absence of sound. It was something with thickness, something that pressed softly against the ears.\nThe silence of the city is made by subtraction. The soundlessness produced by double-glazed windows and soundproof walls is quiet, to be sure, but it says nothing. The silence of a snowy morning is different. It is the presence of what has piled up, the news that the world has changed its shape over the course of one night. The silence itself can be heard.\nThis morning too, the light through the curtains is white. If I opened them, there would probably be a thin layer of snow. I do not hurry to the window; for a while I stay in my futon, listening to that silence. For just these few minutes of hearing what cannot be heard, my Tokyo room becomes continuous with my faraway hometown.",
