    "translation": "Search for reactions to novels and you will often find complaints like 'the ending just abandons everything' or 'too many plot threads are never resolved.' I understand the feeling. Having kept company with a story for a long time, it is natural to want to see how things turn out.\nBut an ending in which everything is resolved, while a kindness to the reader, is also the safest possible way of finishing a story. When the mystery is solved and the characters' fates are settled, the reader closes the book with peace of mind. This 'closing' carries a double meaning. At the same moment the cover is closed, the story is also closed off inside the reader's heart. A story that has served its purpose is tidied neatly away, and in time is recalled less and less.\nThere is a novel I read twenty years ago that I still think about from time to time. The story ends abruptly at the scene where the protagonist silently leaves town. Not a single line tells us what became of him afterward. At the time, I was irritated. But before I knew it, for these twenty years I have been imagining his afterlife on my own, keeping him alive. Precisely because no ending was given, the story cannot finish inside me.\nWhat is left unexplained is not necessarily the author's laziness. It is a form of trust in the reader's imagination — a space left open so that the work can go on living inside the reader.",
