    "translation": "In the evening, when the higurashi cicadas began to cry, I would feel a small jolt as a child. That clear, faintly mournful call — kana-kana — was the signal that the day called today was drawing toward its end. If the cry of the large brown cicada is a sound that boils down the heat of midsummer, the higurashi's cry was the sound of the day's edge cooling into dusk. To a body absorbed in play, that voice announced from outside: it is time to go home.\nFor a child with no watch and only a vague sense of time, the day was portioned out by the higurashi. When their call sounded, I climbed out of the river and hurried homeward with wet feet. Looking back, the surface of the river shone copper in the setting sun. Not because I would be scolded. It was because walking a darkening road while that voice rang out was almost unbearably lonely. Looking back, I suppose it was through the higurashi's cry that I learned the feel of endings. Happy hours always end. I learned that not through reasoning but through my ears.\nAs an adult I manage my days with a wristwatch and a schedule. Time has become numbers, and endings are announced by notification sounds. The end of a meeting, a manuscript deadline — the numbers in the corner of a screen and an alarm tell me everything. Convenient, yes — but neither numbers nor notifications carry that loneliness. Time simply passes with precision, stripped of the feeling of mourning an ending.\nThe other day, in a small town in the mountains where I was on a business trip, I heard the higurashi for the first time in years. My feet stopped, and deep in my chest I was suddenly a child again. In the gathering dusk, that clear two-beat call had not changed in the slightest. To stand still and listen to a voice announcing an ending — that was something I had forgotten for a very long time.",
