    "translation": "A novel I was devoted to at twenty, I reread after passing forty. The result was strange. The protagonist's rebellion, which had once thrilled me, now looked somehow childish. In its place, the silence of a middle-aged man — a minor character I had skimmed past back then — stopped my page-turning hand again and again. It was as if I were reading the book for the first time. The text has not changed by a single character. What changed, needless to say, is me.\nA book, I think, resembles a musical score. The printed notes are immovable, yet the music differs with every performance. In reading, the performer is the reader — and what is more, the reader's very instrument changes with the years. If the middle-aged man's silence was inaudible to me at twenty, it was because the me of those days did not yet possess that register. Experience adds strings; loss deepens the tone. An instrument grows only through time that has been lived.\nThat is why I am a little suspicious of people who say their impression of a book has never changed in their whole life. Either they have not reread it, or, rereading it, they are merely tracing over their own former impressions. Rereading is not a task of confirming an old thrill. Just as one who steps into the same river twice touches different water the second time, one who opens the same book ought to meet a different reading.\nIf I cannot bring myself to clear out the books on my shelves, it is not from lingering attachment. Those are not books I have finished reading; they are books waiting for the next me to read them. That novel I read at twenty will surely show yet another face when the me of sixty reads it. A good book amounts to many books in one. Its thickness is measured not in pages but in the length of the reader's life.",
