    "translation": "In a museum, I stood before a still life. Three apples on a table — no plate, simply set there, nothing more. Nor was it the work of a famous painter. And yet I found I could not take my eyes off those apples. It is a strange business, if you think about it. Real apples could be lying around my kitchen and I would not spare them a glance. Why is it only before painted apples that a person stops?\nAfter gazing a while, I hit upon one answer. The painter must have stared at these apples for tens of hours. The green lurking in the red of the skin, the reflected light inside the shadow, the slight distortion of the outline. Into the layers of paint, that time of concentrated staring has been worked. What I am looking at is not apples but a record of the act of looking at apples. And so whoever stands before the picture borrows the painter's eyes and finds himself looking at things many times more slowly than usual.\nIn everyday life, we do not actually see things. An apple is processed as a sign with the label 'fruit' attached, and the gaze slides across its surface and moves on. The moment we know the name, we stop seeing. If still lifes stop us in our tracks, it is not because rare things are depicted in them. It is because ordinary things are held out to us in the form they had before they became signs.\nLeaving the museum, I saw apples heaped high at a fruit shop, catching the evening sun. For a little while, I looked at them with their name forgotten. Though the painting's effect lasted only a few minutes.",
