    "translation": "In the middle of packing for a move, a diary from around age twenty surfaced from the bottom of a cardboard box. I stopped working and began to read, and within a few pages my face was burning. Overblown anguish, borrowed ideas, a style that seemed to carry all the world's misfortune on its single pair of shoulders. The urge to rip it up and the urge to burst out laughing welled up together. The person who wrote this is unmistakably me — and yet is no longer me.\nWhat was strange was that the 'I' in the diary contradicted my present memory. The twenty-year-old in my memory is somewhat calmer, a somewhat more decent person. The inconvenient impatience and jealousy had, at some point, been shaved away in the process of being arranged into the shape called reminiscence. The diary permits no such editing. In the ink of that time, in the unruly handwriting of that time, what should have been shaved away remains exactly as it was.\nAs I read on, the embarrassment gradually changed into a different feeling. This young man, clumsy as he was, was serious. Night after night he kneaded questions that had no answers, wrote in borrowed words because he had none of his own, and still did not stop writing. My present composure merely rests on top of that ungainly seriousness.\nI did not throw the diary away; I placed it on the bottom shelf of the bookcase in the new house. I will probably not reread it for a good while. But when memory flatters me too much, that unruly handwriting will correct it. One's past self, perhaps, is not an opponent to be overcome but a witness one goes to see from time to time.",
