    "translation": "From a desk drawer in my family home came a bundle of letters I had exchanged with a friend in our university days. The rubber band had perished and snapped; the corners of the envelopes were worn round. The blue ink has blurred here and there, and yet the characters still stand in tidy rows, just as they did thirty years ago. Back then he had gone on to a university in a northern town, and I was a student in Tokyo. Reluctant to spend money on phone calls, we exchanged letters of several pages two or three times a month.\nRereading them, what strikes me is how 'waiting time' is woven all through their lines. 'By the time this letter reaches you, will the snow on your side have started to melt?' The writer knew that the reader would read this several days later, and chose his words with that time lag in mind. And during the ten days or more of waiting for a reply, I pictured his room and his expression again and again. Even while I waited, the letter was still going on. A letter, I now realize, was a gift that included not only the words on the paper but this time of imagining as well.\nThese days my exchanges with him too have shrunk to short messages traded on the spot. Send one and it arrives in seconds, and the 'read' mark appears. I have no complaint about the convenience. Nor do I mean to say that words have become cheap. Only, words that arrive instantly leave no room for waiting. Those few days spent thinking of the other person — that suspended, in-between time — may in fact have been what gave words their weight.\nAt the very bottom of the bundle was one unfinished letter that had apparently never been posted. Instead of sending it, I went out to buy letter paper for the first time in thirty years. I had begun to want to write, once more, words that would not arrive right away.",
