    "translation": "In the garden of my family home stands a large persimmon tree. I am told my grandfather planted it, but that grandfather died before I was born, and I know neither his face nor his voice. When I was a child, every autumn my mother would knock down the fruit with a bamboo pole, remove the astringency, and hand them around the neighborhood. To me the persimmon tree was part of a landscape that had simply always been there; I had no real sense that anyone had planted it.\nThe other day, a gardener who had come to tend the garden laid a hand on the trunk and said, 'This one is a good sixty years old.' According to my mother, my grandfather planted the sapling a few years before he died. The persimmon is a tree that takes years to bear fruit. The old saying 'peach and chestnut three years, persimmon eight' teaches the planter that the harvest will not necessarily be his own. The fruit my grandfather got to taste, if any, must have been little. Even so, he planted. Knowing he would not be there when it bore fruit — for the generation of grandchildren he would never meet.\nWe live in an age that favors things with quick results. Whatever effort we invest, we would prefer to recoup while we are still alive to see it. Measured with the yardstick of efficiency, there can be few jobs as unprofitable as planting a tree. But the persimmon in the garden stands outside that arithmetic. The one who planted it is long gone, yet the fruit alone faithfully appears year after year, and people who never knew the planter eat it. That an act and its fruits need not be completed within a single lifetime — this the tree goes on silently demonstrating.\nThis autumn, for the first time, I picked the fruit with my own hands. When I bit into it, the time of the grandfather I never met was unmistakably sweet. I too would like to plant a seed somewhere — one whose fruiting I need not stay to see.",
